{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0134VTcC14czeQHZkAhfupVF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The pipeline: extract specs from C source → specify (SRD) → synthesize (AI code gen) → verify (differential testing).\n\n## Current State Analysis\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` field, I need to find which SRDs have requirements with status \"ready\" — these are the ones I should propose tasks for.\n\nThe requirement_states shows:\n\n1. **srd004-ts** — All requirements R1.1 through R10.3 are \"ready\" (ts utility, release 15.0)\n\n\n2. **srd105-stty** — All requirements R1.1 through R7.3 are \"ready\" (stty utility, release 15.0)\n3. **srd106-df** — Requirements R3.1 through R4.3 are \"ready\" (df utility, release 13.0)\n4. **srd108-vdir** — Requirements R2.3, R2.4 are \"ready\" (vdir utility, release 13.0)\n5. **srd109-dircolors** — All requirements R1.1 through R3.5 are \"ready\" (dircolors utility, release 13.0)\n6. **srd110-pr** — All requirements R1.1 through R5.2 are \"ready\" (pr utility, release 15.0)\n7. **srd111-ptx** — All requirements R1.1 through R5.2 are \"ready\" (ptx utility, release 15.0)\n8. **srd112-chronic** — All requirements R1.1 through R2.3 are \"ready\" (chronic utility, release 14.0)\n9. **srd113-pee** — All requirements R1.1 through R2.2 are \"ready\" (pee utility, release 14.0)\n10. **srd114-vidir** — All requirements R1.1 through R2.2 are \"ready\" (vidir utility, release 14.0)\n\nNow I need to look at the release priority constraints to see which of these utilities should be prioritized.\n\nLooking at the code status across releases, I notice that most earlier releases have incomplete use cases. The foundational infrastructure items like testutils and format don't have their requirements marked as ready, so I can't propose tasks for those. Moving through the releases, I see that earlier versions like 00.0, 01.1, 03.1, 04.0, 04.1, and 05.0 all have incomplete utilities without ready requirements, which means I should focus on the earliest release that actually has ready requirements to work with. Continuing through the remaining sections, I see versions 10.0 through 12.1 are all partial with various utilities not yet started, while 13.0 and 14.0 show some progress with specific packages having ready requirements across different ranges, and 15.0 appears to have no entries.\n\nLooking at the actual status more carefully though — vdir and dir are marked as not started in 13.0, but the recent commits show vdir has already been partially implemented with multiple requirement ranges completed, so there's a discrepancy between what the code_status indicates and what the git history shows.\n\nFor the other commands, df has requirements R3.1 through R4.3 ready, and dircolors has R1.1 through R3.5 ready, so 13.0 is definitely the earliest release with ready requirements to work on.\n\nI'm realizing I need the weights from requirements.yaml to properly budget the work, but I can't access files directly. The constraints ask me to list weights before proposing tasks, but that information isn't available to me without reading the files. can include up to 4 requirements per task. Some requirements might have weight greater than 1 though, and since I can't access the requirements.yaml file directly, I'll need to be conservative. The release 15.0 notes mention that weight-4 requirements in srd004-ts, srd105-stty, srd110-pr, and srd111-ptx are each in their own group, so those would need to be solo tasks. For release 13.0, I'm looking at srd108-vdir with requirements R2.3 and R2.4, which are likely weight 1 each since vdir isn't in the later releases with the heavier requirements.\n\nFor srd106-df with its 10 requirements, I need to split them across multiple tasks while respecting the weight limit. I'm grouping R3.1 through R3.4 as the first task, R3.5 through R3.7 as the second, and R4.1 through R4.3 as the third. Now I'm moving on to srd109-dircolors, which has requirements R1.1 through R2.2 that I need to organize similarly.\n\nFor srd109-dircolors with 15 total requirements, I'm breaking them into four tasks: R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4, R2.5-R3.3, and R3.4-R3.5. But I'm hitting a constraint—I can only create at most 4 tasks total across all modules. I need to prioritize based on release timing, starting with the earliest release at 13.0.\n\nLooking at what's left: vdir needs just one small task for R2.3-R2.4, df requires three tasks to cover R3.1-R4.3, and dircolors needs four tasks for all 15 requirements. Option A would let me complete vdir and df entirely with one vdir task plus three df tasks, finishing more of the release. Option B spreads things thinner by adding a dircolors task instead. I'm leaning toward Option A since it delivers more complete coverage, but I should verify whether df and dircolors have any contract dependencies that would affect the ordering. I'm realizing the contract task requirement might not apply to cmd/ packages since they're executables with minimal exported surface area, unlike pkg/ packages which expose types and interfaces. The constraint seems more relevant for library packages, though it does say \"new package\" without making that distinction explicit.\n\nFor df, there's a discrepancy — the code_status shows \"not started\" but the requirement_states only list R3.x and R4.x, suggesting work may have already begun. For dircolors, since it's a cmd/ package and all requirements are marked \"ready,\" I don't think a contract task is necessary here either.\n\nLooking at the recent task history, I only see vdir work completed, so df likely hasn't had any code written yet. The key insight is that requirement_states reflects the current state, and requirements not listed there should be treated as something other than \"ready\" — possibly already complete, in progress, or skipped.\n\nFor srd106-df, only R3.1 through R4.3 are ready, meaning R1.x and R2.x were finished in earlier tasks. For srd108-vdir, R2.3 and R2.4 are the remaining ready items since R1.x and R2.1-R2.2 were already done. For srd109-dircolors, everything from R1.1 through R3.5 is ready since it's a new package.\n\nMy priority order is to tackle vdir's final two requirements first since they're small and will complete the package, then move to df's R3.1 and R3.2.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering whether dircolors needs a contract task before implementation. Looking at the existing vdir tasks in the project, they jumped straight to implementation without a separate contract phase, so cmd/ packages apparently don't require that step. I should follow the same pattern here.\n\nNow I'm realizing the SRD file contents aren't included in the project context, only the requirement IDs and their states. The stitch agent will need to read the SRD file itself, so I should reference the file path in required_reading and specify which requirement IDs to implement. Looking at the recent commit messages, the pattern is straightforward: task name includes the package, SRD file reference, and the specific requirements being implemented.\n\nFor the weight budgets, I can't actually read the requirements.yaml file to see individual weights, but the project memory mentions a max of 4 requirements per task. Since these are 13.0 requirements (not the weight-4 restructured ones from 15.0), I'll be conservative and assume varying weights, so I'll propose at most 4 requirements per task to stay safe.\n\nLooking at my four tasks: Task 0 covers srd108-vdir with R2.3 and R2.4 (the vdir command already exists from prior work), Task 1 handles srd106-df R3.1 through R3.4 (4 requirements for the df command which should already have earlier requirements done), and Task 2 takes srd106-df R3.5 through R3.7 with a dependency on Task 1.\n\nI'm realizing these tasks might actually be independent feature groups within the SRD structure, but since the orchestrator runs them sequentially anyway, the ordering matters for avoiding conflicts when multiple tasks modify the same files. Task 2 should follow Task 1 to ensure the df command builds correctly across both requirement groups. limits. Now I'm setting up the dependency structure: vdir as task 0 with no dependencies, then df tasks chained sequentially since they all modify the same file and need to avoid conflicts.\n\nFor the task descriptions, I'll reference the SRD requirements by their IDs since I can't read the actual spec files directly—the stitch agent will pull those details when it processes the tasks.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific tasks with their file modifications and dependencies. Task 0 handles the remaining vdir requirements, Task 1 covers the first set of df requirements, and Task 2 picks up where Task 1 leaves off with the final df requirements. Task 0 through 3 each have requirements with assumed weights of 1, keeping all tasks within their 4-unit budgets. Since I can't see the actual weight values, I'm being conservative here and the orchestrator will need to validate these assumptions. For the vdir implementation, it should be equivalent to `ls -lbA`.\n\nFor df, I need to check the SRD for the specific disk space reporting requirements. I'm also realizing the stitch agent needs both the SRD file with requirement definitions and the requirements.yaml with weights to understand what to implement. Looking at recent commit patterns, the title format should be \"Task NNNN: [stitch] cmd/name: implement srdNNN R1.1, R1.2\" with the YAML description following separately.\n\nI should verify which files actually exist — vdir's main.go and test file are likely present since it was recently worked on, and df's main.go should exist too since those requirements aren't marked as ready yet. For dir, since no requirements are listed as ready and the code status shows it as not started, I won't create tasks for it. Let me finalize the output now, keeping it focused on what's actually needed. I'm weighing whether to finish the remaining utilities first or start a new one. Completing vdir and continuing df makes more sense than jumping to dircolors, since finishing what's already in progress follows the principle of completing existing work before starting fresh tasks. So I'll go with one vdir task plus three df tasks to hit the four-task limit.\n\nNow I need to think about what files the stitch agent will need to read. For df, the existing cmd/df/main.go file should be included in the required_reading so the agent can build on it. And since vdir is essentially ls with different flags, it likely shares code with the ls implementation, though I'll need to account for the actual file structure—probably cmd/vdir/main.go and its test file—when setting up the task requirements. at the top level or in docs/. The stitch agent doesn't need it directly though — the orchestrator handles weight validation. Looking at the project structure, there's a `configuration.yaml` at the root, so `requirements.yaml` is probably in `docs/specs/` or similar, but that's not critical for what I'm building here.\n\nFor each task, I need to structure it with a deliverable type, required reading files, the actual files to create or modify with their actions and notes, a list of requirements pulled from the SRD IDs, design decisions if applicable, and acceptance criteria. Since I don't have the exact SRD content in front of me, I'll reference the requirement IDs generically and let the stitch agent pull the details from the actual SRD files.\n\nThe key constraint is that each task description needs to be self-contained — the agent won't have context beyond what I write. But since the SRD is in the required reading list, the agent can look up the full details there. I'll frame each requirement as something like \"Implement srd108 R2.3 as specified in the SRD\" so it's clear and actionable, and I need to anchor each SRD requirement to exactly one task requirement per the planning constitution.\n\nThe challenge is I can't actually read the SRD files to know what R2.3, R2.4, or the other requirements say. I could try inferring from the release descriptions — like for df, the filesystem disk space usage with human-readable and SI format options suggests the requirements might cover different output formats or command flags — but I'm better off just referencing the SRD IDs directly and letting the agent read the actual specifications.\n\nFor the design decisions, I need to be careful not to invent anything. The project rules mention standard patterns like SIGPIPE handlers and differential testing for cmd/ packages, but the constraint is clear: only include design decisions that come directly from the SRD or architecture, not guesses about what might be needed. I'm settling on the design decisions: for vdir, I'll extend the existing implementation to handle R2.3-R2.4 with differential tests following the current pattern, and for df, I'll do the same for R3.1-R4.3. Now I need to check the ordering — df should come before vdir alphabetically, so let me make sure I have that right. I'm torn between following the strict alphabetical rule in P8 versus the practical benefit of finishing vdir's smaller task set first. But P8 is explicit—alphabetical ordering by primary file path is the tiebreaker when priorities match. So I'll stick with the df-first ordering, grouping requirements by their natural dependencies: df gets three tasks covering requirements 3.1-4.3, then vdir gets the final task with its two remaining requirements.\n\nNow I'm second-guessing the weight distribution. The constraint limits each task to 4 sub-requirements maximum, and I need to stay under 4 total tasks. Task 0 with R3.1 through R3.4 sits right at the weight boundary