nt will need to read those specifications directly to understand what each requirement demands, so I should point them there clearly and let them extract the specifics rather than trying to guess at what R3.x and R4.x cover without actually reading the document. - Task 3: R2.3, R2.4 (2 requirements)\n\nAll tasks fit within the weight budget of 4 per task. Now I'm generating the YAML configuration and need to determine what required_reading each task should include—the SRD for requirement details, the existing source code to understand current implementations, and any shared packages like pkg/sys and pkg/format.\n\nFor df, I'll reference the SRD, main implementation, tests, and relevant shared packages for syscalls and formatting. For vdir, I'll do the same, plus potentially reference the ls command if vdir shares code with it. The task titles should follow the convention of \"cmd/<name>: implement srd<NNN> R<x.y>, R<x.y>, ...\" to match existing patterns.\n\nI'm also pulling in the standard architectural patterns that apply across all cmd packages—differential testing with a reference binary, graceful skipping when the reference isn't available, and SIGPIPE handling at startup. These are documented in CLAUDE.md and are fair game for design decisions since they come from the architecture rather than being invented.\n\nFor the core design decisions, I'll keep them focused: extending existing implementations rather than rewriting, following the differential testing pattern already in place, and using the appropriate GNU reference binary from Homebrew. For df specifically, that's gdf, and I might need to lean on pkg/sys for filesystem information and pkg/format for readable output. For vdir, it's gvdir and there could be shared code with the ls command.\n\nNow I'm thinking through whether all the df tasks actually need to be chained together—they do modify the same files, so sequential execution makes sense to avoid conflicts.\n\nWriting task structure... All the weight